Solstice Carillon first chimed into existence in the hushed, cavernous spaces of late 80s abandoned observatories and planetariums, frequented by reclusive audiophiles and amateur astronomers. These solitary figures, often dressed in oversized, faded velour tracksuits and vintage space-themed graphic tees, would gather for clandestine listening sessions, projecting star charts onto crumbling walls. The music was their shared secret, a sonic codex inspired by the rare, fleeting moments of cosmic alignment they cherished—a reflection of the almost ritualistic, once-in-a-lifetime performances of their eccentric, sun-worshipping forebears, whose improvised bell compositions only ever materialized during the brief, eerie twilight of a total solar eclipse.

Typical Instruments Used: Yamaha DX7 (especially for bell patches), Roland Juno-106 (for warm pads and arpeggios), E-mu SP-1200 (for subtle, grainy percussive elements), Oberheim OB-Xa (for brassy, sweeping textures), and various custom-sampled bell libraries.

